
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In Emacs 31+, an issue appeared where creating an indirect buffer from a base buffer running a tree-sitter major mode results in a <strong>loss of syntax highlighting</strong>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I reported this issue, but the <strong>Emacs maintainers will not fix it</strong> (hopefully, they will see the benefit of fixing it one day, once they find a way to fix the architecture). This means that users who depend on indirect buffers are left without a solution.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To address this, <strong>I wrote a workaround that fixes syntax highlighting in cloned indirect buffers that use tree-sitter in Emacs &gt;= 31.</strong> (See below.)</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Note: I published this workaround as a temporary fix for indirect buffer users while maintainers hopefully work toward a clean and permanent solution. I agree with the maintainers that we are better off finding a permanent solution instead of merging a hack upstream.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Why are indirect buffers useful</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Indirect buffers are useful because they allow maintaining independent display states for the same file content. For example:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Code folding: you can have the same source code open in two different windows, with one window completely folded to show only the high-level class architecture, while the other window is fully expanded for editing a specific method. (e.g., when using one of the code folding modes <a href="https://github.com/jamescherti/kirigami.el" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kirigami.el</a> supports, for example)</li>



<li>Narrowing: They also allow independent narrowing. For example, the <code>narrow-to-defun</code> function can be used in an indirect buffer to isolate a single function, preventing accidental edits to other parts of the file, while keeping the entire file visible and editable in the base buffer.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The bug</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To reproduce the bug:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Open a source file that uses a tree-sitter major mode (e.g., <code>python-ts-mode</code>).</li>



<li>Create a new window: <code>M-x split-window-right</code> to display the source code across two windows.</li>



<li>Create an indirect buffer using <code>M-x clone-indirect-buffer</code>.</li>



<li>Scroll down and observe that the syntax highlighting is entirely or partially missing in the new indirect buffer.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The workaround</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Since an official fix is not planned (See the technical context below), I wrote a workaround for users who depend on indirect buffers. Add the following Elisp code to your Emacs &gt;= 31 configuration to fix syntax highlighting on indirect buffers:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp"><span class="hljs-comment">;; Author: James Cherti</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">;; License: GPL</span>
<span class="hljs-comment">;; URL: https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/bug-gnu-emacs/2026-08/msg00904.html</span>

(<span class="hljs-name">defun</span> initialize-indirect-buffer-treesit-parsers ()
  <span class="hljs-string">"Initialize tree-sitter parsers in indirect buffers based on the base buffer."</span>
  (<span class="hljs-name">when</span> (<span class="hljs-name">and</span> (<span class="hljs-name">fboundp</span> 'treesit-major-mode-setup)
             (<span class="hljs-name">fboundp</span> 'treesit-parser-list)
             (<span class="hljs-name">fboundp</span> 'treesit-parser-create))
    (<span class="hljs-name">let*</span> ((<span class="hljs-name">base</span> (<span class="hljs-name">buffer-base-buffer</span>))
           <span class="hljs-comment">;; If this is an indirect buffer, switch context to the base buffer</span>
           <span class="hljs-comment">;; to retrieve its active parsers.</span>
           (<span class="hljs-name">parser-list</span> (<span class="hljs-name">and</span> base
                             (<span class="hljs-name">with-current-buffer</span> base
                               (<span class="hljs-name">treesit-parser-list</span>)))))
      (<span class="hljs-name">when</span> parser-list
        <span class="hljs-comment">;; Instantiate identical parsers for this indirect buffer. </span>
        (<span class="hljs-name">dolist</span> (<span class="hljs-name">parser</span> parser-list)
          (<span class="hljs-name">treesit-parser-create</span> (<span class="hljs-name">treesit-parser-language</span> parser)))

        <span class="hljs-comment">;; Activate tree-sitter features (such as font-lock and indentation).</span>
        (<span class="hljs-name">treesit-major-mode-setup</span>)

        <span class="hljs-comment">;; Force an immediate font-lock refresh</span>
        (<span class="hljs-name">when</span> (<span class="hljs-name">fboundp</span> 'font-lock-flush)
          (<span class="hljs-name">font-lock-flush</span>))))))

(<span class="hljs-name">when</span> (<span class="hljs-name">&gt;=</span> emacs-major-version <span class="hljs-number">31</span>)
  (<span class="hljs-name">add-hook</span> 'clone-indirect-buffer-hook #'initialize-indirect-buffer-treesit-parsers))</code></span></pre>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The context: bug #81637</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">According to the <a href="https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/bug-gnu-emacs/2026-08/msg00904.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bug#81637</a> discussion, Emacs 30 shared the tree-sitter parser between the base buffer and the indirect buffer. This caused bugs with narrowing. If the base buffer was narrowed, the indirect buffer could see the whole text, but the shared parser only saw the narrowed portion.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To resolve the narrowing bugs, the architecture was changed so that indirect and base buffers maintain separate parser lists. However, when an indirect buffer is cloned, the major mode function is not executed.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While discussing potential fixes, it became clear that an Emacs upstream resolution is unlikely. One of the Emacs maintainers noted that full support for indirect buffers is difficult and advised against relying on them, stating that the Emacs does not officially support font-lock in indirect buffers.</p>
